Subject: [PATCH] x86/time: avoid unavailable legacy clockevents in virtual guests

WHP guests have a local APIC timer but no legacy timer IRQ. Keeping
global_clock_event after request_irq() fails makes LAPIC calibration verify
against jiffies that never advance, disabling the working LAPIC timer.
Clear the unavailable reference so calibration uses the supplied TSC frequency.

Virtual guests with a known TSC frequency and an always-running local APIC
can calibrate the APIC timer by polling the TSC. Avoid initializing a legacy
clockevent in that case: IRQ registration may succeed even when the VMM has
no PIT, leaving verification to reject a working APIC timer against stalled
jiffies. Keep PIT fallback when frequencies or a usable local APIC are absent.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/time.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/time.c
@@ -49,8 +49,10 @@ static void __init setup_default_timer_irq(void)
 	 * without legacy PIC/PIT we need this for the HPET0 in legacy
 	 * replacement mode.
 	 */
-	if (request_irq(0, timer_interrupt, flags, "timer", NULL))
+	if (request_irq(0, timer_interrupt, flags, "timer", NULL)) {
 		pr_info("Failed to register legacy timer interrupt\n");
+		global_clock_event = NULL;
+	}
 }
 
 /* Default timer init function */

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/apic/apic.c
@@ -781,6 +781,9 @@
 	/* APIC timer disabled? */
 	if (disable_apic_timer)
 		return true;
+	/* Virtual guests can calibrate the always-running APIC timer against TSC. */
+	if (boot_cpu_has(X86_FEATURE_HYPERVISOR))
+		return false;
 	/*
 	 * The APIC timer frequency is known already, no PIT calibration
 	 * required. If unknown, let the PIT be initialized.
